'''Swarm intelligence''' describes collective problem solving or coordinated behavior arising from interactions among many relatively simple individuals. Researchers study natural examples and engineer algorithms inspired by them.

== Computational mechanisms ==
In particle swarm optimization (PSO), particles have positions x_i and velocities v_i; personal best p_i and neighborhood best g_i guide motion. A common update is <math>v_i\leftarrow\omega v_i+c_1r_1(p_i-x_i)+c_2r_2(g_i-x_i)</math>, followed by x_i ← x_i+v_i. The r variables are sampled independently in common implementations. Ant colony optimization instead uses probabilistic construction guided by heuristic desirability and a shared, evaporating pheromone-like state. These algorithms use different mechanisms and assumptions.

== Reproducible comparison ==
For a bounded optimization problem, initialize particles using recorded random seeds, evaluate the objective, update best positions and velocities, handle boundary violations explicitly and stop at a fixed evaluation budget. Compare best objective, median performance and failure rates across independent runs against random search and appropriate deterministic baselines. Tune parameters on separate instances; a favorable benchmark does not imply universal superiority.

== Biological and engineering limits ==
Natural collective behavior includes sensory, ecological and evolutionary mechanisms beyond algorithm analogies. Swarm robotics faces communication range, localization error, collision risk, energy budgets and physical failures. Local rules can produce stagnation, congestion or synchronization of errors. Collective performance does not establish consciousness or human-like reasoning.

